"I want to enforce a guardrail to terminate a session if an agent clicks on a link that is outside of an allowed list of domains. See claude proposal: When running an autonomous agent.execute() loop, the agent can freely navigate to any URL. The goto tool calls page.goto(url) with no validation ( goto.ts:25 ), as does the CUA handler ( v3CuaAgentHandler.ts:573 ). There is currently no built-in way to constrain an agent to a set of approved domains, or to terminate a session when it \"wanders\" off-domain (via a tool call, an HTTP redirect, or JS-driven window.location changes). For regulated / security-sensitive automation, letting an LLM-driven agent navigate to arbitrary domains is a real risk (data exfiltration to an unexpected host, following a malicious link/redirect, credential entry on a look-alike domain). Today the only mitigation is to roll your own check. Describe the solution you'd like A first-class config option to declare allowed domains and a policy for what happens on violation. Proposed shape: const stagehand = new Stagehand({ // ... navigationGuardrail: { allowedDomains: [\"example.com\", \"*.example.com\"], // exact + wildcard subdomains onViolation: \"abort\", // \"abort\" | \"block\" | \"warn\" }, }); \"abort\" — terminate the run, surfacing the existing AgentAbortError with a descriptive reason. \"block\" — cancel the navigation/keep the agent on the current page and feed an error back to the model. \"warn\" — log only. The most robust enforcement seam is Page.onFrameNavigated ( page.ts:456 ), since it catches all navigation — agent goto calls, redirects, and client-side location changes — not just tool invocations. CDP request interception (Fetch.enable / Network.setBlockedURLs, not currently used in the codebase) would enable true preventive \"block\" behavior. Describe alternatives you've considered The hooks exist to approximate this today, but each has gaps: signal + onStepFinish ( agent.ts:305 , agent.ts:126 ): check page.url() after each step and call controller.abort() if off-domain. Works, but is reactive — the off-domain page has already loaded, and the check only runs at step boundaries, so one off-domain action can slip through before the abort. It also misses mid-step redirects. const controller = new AbortController(); const ALLOWED = new Set([\"example.com\"]); await agent.execute({ instruction: \"...\", signal: controller.signal, callbacks: { onStepFinish: async () => { const host = new URL((await stagehand.page).url()).hostname; if (![...ALLOWED].some(d => host === d || host.endsWith(\".\" + d))) controller.abort( Off-domain navigation blocked: ${host} ); }, }, }); Validate inside the goto tool / CUA goto case — preventive for tool-driven navigation, but a fork is required and it still misses redirects and JS navigation. A built-in guardrail at the navigation layer would cover all of these uniformly and be safe-by-default for security-conscious users."
